g Table 1, we have already observed that the hyperbolic Minkowski space (generated through the normalising Euclidean metric, R_{n}) is the entropic analogue to the Euclidean Minkowski space of kinematics, with consequent del operators; that Boltzmann’s constant is the entropic quantum analogue to Planck’s quantum of action (also pointed out by Córdoba et al.^{37}) with consequently analogous definitions for momentum; and that the helical pitch (or wavelength) implies the space-like entropic analogue of time in kinematics. ... The parameter Λ describing a logarithmic spiral contributes to the entropic (hyperbolic) acceleration Γ as a consequence of an entropic force, in analogy to Newton’s 2^{nd} Law; and the double-helix can be seen as a special case (Λ = 0) of the double-armed logarithmic spiral.\nOpen in a new tab\n\nConsidering Table 1, we have already observed that the hyperbolic Minkowski space (generated through the normalising Euclidean metric, R_{n}) is the entropic analogue to the Euclidean Minkowski space of kinematics, with consequent del operators; that Boltzmann’s constant is the entropic quantum analogue to Planck’s quantum of action (also pointed out by Córdoba et al.^{37}) with consequently analogous definitions for momentum; and that the helical pitch (or wavelength) implies the space-like entropic analogue of time in kinematics. As we have seen, this latter also implies holographic properties of the treatment (that is, properties of an area being fully equivalent to parameters of a volume).\n\nBoth mass and its entropic equivalent m_{S} have natural units of inverse length, but m_{S} is imaginary as a consequence of the holomorphism (Eq. 9c). The parameter Λ describing a logarithmic spiral contributes to the entropic (hyperbolic) acceleration Γ as a consequence of an entropic force, in analogy to Newton’s 2^{nd} Law; and the double-helix can be seen as a special case (Λ = 0) of the double-armed logarithmic spiral. The Hamiltonian and Lagrangian formulations then follow equivalently for both energy and entropy, with the Exertion integral equivalent to the classical Action integral, both obeying the principle of stationary “action”.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSearch | Global Science Press (https://www.global-sci.com/index/search?query=Ting+Chen)\nciteturn3search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; P2¡ÁP2 Jun Li, Yang Zhou Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2309-2316 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-019-1599-4 On spectral theory of the Riemann zeta function Xian-Jin Li Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2317-2330 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-018-9356-0 Logarithmic vanishing theorems for effective q-ample divisors Kefeng Liu, Xueyuan Wan, Xiaokui Yang Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2331-2334 https://doi.org/10.1007/S11425-019-9553-2 Rigidity of certain admissible pairs of rational homogeneous spaces of Picard number 1 which are not of the subdiagram type Ngaiming Mok Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2335-2354 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-019-1592-6 Semilinear automorphisms of classical groups and quivers Jinwei Yang, Zhiwei Yun Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2355-2370 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-019-1612-8 Quantitative gradient estimates for harmonic maps into singular spaces Hui-Chun Zhang, Xiao Zhong, Xi-Ping Zhu Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2371-2400 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-018-9493-1 Linear forms, algebraic cycles, and derivatives of L-series Shouwu Zhang Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2401-2408 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-019-1589-7 The arithmetic fundamental lemma: An update Wei Zhang Sci China Math, 62(11), 2019, pp. 2409-2422 https://doi.org/10.1007/s11425-019-9559-4 Some recent progress in non-K?\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nWave Propagation on Microstate Geometries | Annales Henri Poincaré | Springer Nature Link (https://springerlink.fh-diploma.de/article/10.1